{
	title: 'Background bash: result arrives as a message',
	spec: "The bash tool takes an optional background: true (questions.md item 29). The command starts at once in its own process group; the call waits at most 100 ms, so a command that fails at once (not found, syntax error, exit within 100 ms) returns its output and exit code as a normal result; otherwise it returns 'started in background as #<call block id>' and the turn goes on. When the command exits, its output (capped and saved like a normal bash result) and exit code reach the session as an advisory message from 'bash #<call block id>', delivered like messages from other sessions (task rj): into the running turn at the next tool result, or starting a turn when the session is idle, except while the user has it paused. The background job uses the already recorded call block number as its visible id, not a random hex id; its advisory message links back to that call in terminal and web. Bash call descriptions and the command occupy separate lines, a foreground command prefixed with $ and a background command with &. Both clients omit a redundant [exit 0] in display (not in history/provider input) and colour only the [exit N] token for a nonzero exit; the rest of the error remains in the ordinary text colour. Escape does not kill background commands; closing the session or quitting the last Hal process does, as for foreground ones. Background commands survive neither a host restart nor a takeover: after one, the session gets 'bash #<call block id> was lost when Hal restarted'.",
	status: 'done',
	needs: [
		'1r',
		'rj'
	],
	foldInto: [
		'1r'
	],
	notes: [
		'Background call references use the last matching recorded assistant tool-call n (history.readSync, already cached in an open session) and an internal key <session>:<n>; legacy b-hex ids in saved metadata still display unchanged on recovery. src/common/bash-result.ts changes presentation only, not the recorded tool result or advisory content.',
		'Process running for both modes lives in src/host/jobs.ts (exec, plus the background registry); tools/bash.ts only validates and picks a mode. tools.cap is the shared output cut, so the delivered message is capped like a result.',
		"The message is prompts.submit with Sender { from: <the session itself>, label: 'bash #<call block id>', advisory: true }; from set means it waits while paused/failed and never promotes a subagent. subagents.owed treats from === self like a subagent's report: the turn it starts inherits who owns the work.",
		"Background calls get no default timeout (only an explicit one) and ignore the turn's abort signal, including during the 100 ms grace wait.",
		"Running ids are kept in session.ason `background` (validated as a list of strings). host.init's exit handler (and host.reset) calls jobs.killAll without clearing it, so main.becomeHost's jobs.lost() finds the ids of open tabs and busy sessions and sends 'bash #<call block id> was lost when Hal restarted'. Tab close (tabs.close) kills silently and clears it; closing a tab never stopped foreground turns, so this is the only kill on close.",
		"subagents.finished does not close a subagent's tab while it has background commands, since closing would kill them.",
		'The note saying closing a tab kills only background commands was superseded by task 8v: tab close pauses the foreground turn and owned subagent turns too.'
	]
}
